meta {
  name: Resolve including proposals and ancestors
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/curriculum-nodes/{{curriculumTopicId}}/content?include_proposed=true&include_ancestors=true
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.count: eq 2
  res.body.include_proposed: eq true
  res.body.include_ancestors: eq true
  res.body.counts.placements: eq 2
  res.body.counts.exact: eq 1
  res.body.counts.readable: eq 2
}

docs {
  The same topic, asked with both fallbacks on, which is what a review screen
  wants rather than what a pupil's question should get.

  include_ancestors walks up the tree from this topic to its unit and on to the
  curriculum, and adds the curriculum-grain placements alongside. That is how a
  topic nobody mapped still finds the book its syllabus was taught from.

  include_proposed adds mappings nobody has agreed with yet. Useful when
  checking an import, wrong for answering a pupil: a guess presented as an
  answer is worse than no answer.

  exact tells the two apart in the result. One row here is exact -- it names
  this very node -- and the other came from the curriculum-grain fallback.
  Counting exact separately is what lets a client say "from your syllabus
  point" instead of "from somewhere in this subject".

  A node carrying more than 500 placements is refused with 409
  placements_unbounded rather than truncated. A truncated list would search a
  subset of the mapped content without saying so, and the caller would have no
  way to tell a thin answer from a complete one.
}
